menuconfig STUN
	bool "STUN (Session Traversal Utilities for NAT) library [EXPERIMENTAL]"
	select EXPERIMENTAL
	select PSA_CRYPTO
	select CRC
	select UTF8
	imply PSA_WANT_ALG_HMAC
	imply PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_HMAC
	imply PSA_WANT_ALG_SHA_1
	imply PSA_WANT_ALG_SHA_256
	help
	  STUN message codec as defined in RFC 8489: parsing and building of
	  messages, the MESSAGE-INTEGRITY and MESSAGE-INTEGRITY-SHA256
	  attributes (computed through the PSA Crypto API), FINGERPRINT and
	  the attributes ICE (RFC 8445) adds. The library does no I/O.

	  Both integrity attributes need HMAC of the PSA Crypto API,
	  MESSAGE-INTEGRITY with SHA-1 and MESSAGE-INTEGRITY-SHA256 with
	  SHA-256. All of it is enabled by default. The functions that
	  compute or verify an attribute return -EIO if the backend lacks
	  what the attribute needs.

if STUN

module = STUN
module-dep = NET_LOG
module-str = Log level for STUN
module-help = Enable the log of the STUN library.
source "subsys/net/Kconfig.template.log_config.net"

config STUN_CLIENT
	bool "STUN client over UDP sockets"
	depends on NET_SOCKETS && NET_UDP
	select CSPRNG_NEEDED
	help
	  The Binding transaction, which does no I/O, and blocking calls that
	  run it over a UDP socket to ask a STUN server for the address it
	  sees this device at, on a socket of the caller or on one opened for
	  the purpose. The transaction id of a request has to be unpredictable
	  (RFC 8489 section 5), so the client needs a cryptographically
	  secure random number generator. A server given by host name, not
	  by literal address, also needs DNS_RESOLVER.

if STUN_CLIENT

config STUN_CLIENT_SOFTWARE
	string "SOFTWARE attribute of the client's requests"
	default "Zephyr"
	help
	  What the client describes itself as to the server (RFC 8489
	  sections 6.1 and 14.14), at most 96 bytes. Leave it empty to send
	  no SOFTWARE attribute; a version number here tells an observer what
	  to attack.

config STUN_CLIENT_RX_BUF_SIZE
	int "Size of the client's receive buffer"
	default 576
	range 128 1500
	help
	  The client receives into a buffer of this size on the stack of its
	  caller. A STUN message over UDP is meant to stay below 576 bytes
	  (RFC 8489 section 6.1); a response to a Binding request is some
	  100 to 200 bytes with the attributes servers add to it. A datagram
	  that does not fit is not taken for an answer, and the query ends
	  with a timeout.

config STUN_BINDING_RTO_MS
	int "Initial retransmission timeout of a Binding request, in milliseconds"
	default 500
	range 500 60000
	help
	  How long a Binding transaction waits for the answer before it sends
	  the request a second time. The wait doubles with every further
	  transmission (RFC 8489 section 6.2.1, which asks for 500 ms or
	  more).

config STUN_BINDING_MAX_TRANSMITS
	int "Number of times a Binding request is sent"
	default 7
	range 1 16
	help
	  Rc of RFC 8489 section 6.2.1: how many times a Binding transaction
	  sends its request, the first transmission included, while no answer
	  arrives.

config STUN_BINDING_FINAL_WAIT_RTO
	int "Wait after the last transmission, in initial timeouts"
	default 16
	range 1 255
	help
	  Rm of RFC 8489 section 6.2.1: after its last transmission a Binding
	  transaction waits this many times STUN_BINDING_RTO_MS for the
	  answer and then fails. With the defaults of the three options a
	  transaction that gets no answer lasts 39.5 seconds.

endif # STUN_CLIENT

config STUN_SHELL
	bool "STUN shell commands"
	depends on STUN_CLIENT && NET_SHELL
	help
	  Add "net stun" to the network shell: "net stun server" names the
	  STUN server to ask, "net stun query" asks it for the address it
	  sees this device at and prints the answer. The query runs in the
	  thread of the shell and takes some 2.5 KB of its stack on a 32-bit
	  target, so SHELL_STACK_SIZE defaults to 4096 with this option.

endif # STUN
